Short-circuit evaluation stops once the result is known: `a && b` skips `b` if `a` is `false`; `a || b` skips `b` if `a` is `true`. Routinely used to guard null-dereference:

```js
if (user !== null && user.isAdmin) {
  grantAccess();
}
```

De Morgan's applies directly: `!(a && b)` becomes `!a || !b`, so negating this condition gives `user === null || !user.isAdmin` — more readable, same order-safe evaluation.
